### colonizer --help
turn a task into a pull request: a coding agent works in an isolated sandbox, watched from a cockpit

Usage: colonizer [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  version      Print what this build is, and whether it is a release (also `--version`)
  about        Print what the venture is built with, each product's status said in words (issue #944)
  help         Print this message, or a command's own help; `help glossary` explains this CLI's words in ordinary language (issue #904)
  update       Install the newest release against a running mothership and restart into it
  setup        Install this version's release over a `cargo install` build, which has no app beside it
  open         Print the cockpit sign-in link and open it in a browser
  login-item   Start the control plane (the mothership) at login (macOS LaunchAgent, Linux systemd user unit)
  telemetry    Show or change anonymous usage reporting (no network, no daemon needed)
  hotspots     Show the files merged pull requests touched most often in a window — where parallel colonies collide, and a hint at what to split (issue #831)
  sessions     Manage this machine's session store: where colonies' records and logs live (docs/session-store.md)
  completions  Print a shell completion script for this command (source it from your shell's rc)
  man          Print this command's man page to stdout
  launch       Start a colony: an isolated sandbox running one coding agent, working the repo or one issue in it
  handoff      Hand a local agent session to a colony: a session you ran here continues on the mothership
  list         List the colonies this token may see, newest first
  status       Show one colony (an isolated sandbox): where it stands, what it costs, and what it is doing right now
  logs         Print a colony's recent events, or follow them live
  diff         Print everything a colony has changed against its base branch, as a unified diff
  ask          Print the question a colony is waiting on, with its options numbered
  answer       Answer a colony's pending question: an option's number, its label, or free text
  stop         Stop a colony: the microVM goes away, the worktree is kept for a later `resume`
  resume       Start a stopped colony again, picking up its worktree where it was left
  pr           Print a colony's pull request URL and state, or --wait for its checks to settle
  map          Print a repository's architecture map as a text outline, or search it with --find
  mcp          Serve this harness to MCP clients over stdio (tools for listing, watching and driving colonies); the tool set follows the token's scope
  loop         Manage loops: saved prompts that launch a colony on a schedule — the cockpit's Loops page from a terminal. `colonizer loop create --help` shows the cadence grammar
  redteam      Start and list red-team runs: hunters that find and report bugs, or security defects with `--preset security`
  token        Manage the control plane's (the mothership's) scoped API tokens (the owner token only)
  fleet        Export this machine's stats, logs and colony history, or import another machine's (issue #687)

Options:
      --host <HOST[:PORT]>  Which mothership the client commands talk to: a host name or host:port. The default is the local one (COLONIZER_BIND, else 127.0.0.1:7878); across a tailnet or tunnel, name it
      --token-file <PATH>   File holding the API token (the value itself, one line). The resolution order is COLONIZER_TOKEN, then this file, then the local <config_dir>/api-token
      --json                Print machine-readable JSON instead of the human rendering, where a command has one
  -h, --help                Print help
  -V, --version             Print version

With no subcommand at all, colonizer starts the control plane (the mothership): it serves the web
UI and the API on COLONIZER_BIND (default 127.0.0.1:7878) and runs the colonies.

New to the words? `colonizer help glossary` says what a colony, a settler and the mothership
actually are, in ordinary language.

Exit codes:
  0  ok
  1  error: the mothership is unreachable, refused, or something else went wrong
  2  usage: the arguments name no command this build knows
  3  unauthorized or forbidden: the token is missing, unknown (401) or not allowed (403)
  4  not found: no such colony, loop or token (404)
  5  conflict (409), or `ask`/`answer` on a colony that is not asking anything
  6  a launch cap was refused (429)
  7  `pr --wait` followed the checks and they failed
  8  `pr --wait --timeout` ran out of time before the checks settled

Settings come from the environment, not flags: COLONIZER_BIND, COLONIZER_DATA_DIR,
COLONIZER_HOME and the rest are in docs/install.md. The mothership and the local commands
(`open`, `login-item`, `telemetry`, `sessions migrate`) read them. The client commands take --host and --token-file,
and so does `update` — a thin client of a running mothership; the other local commands refuse
them (and --json), which only the client commands use.

### colonizer help glossary
Plain-language glossary

This CLI describes itself in metaphors. Here is each one next to the ordinary words for it.

  isolated sandbox (colony)
      A throwaway microVM — a tiny virtual machine with its own kernel — in which one
      coding agent works on a repository. Nothing it does reaches the machine you sit
      at: it has its own filesystem, its own credentials and its own network rules, and
      stopping it throws the whole thing away. `launch` starts one; `list`, `status`,
      `logs` and `diff` read what it did; `stop` ends it and `resume` picks its worktree
      back up where it was left.

  worker agent (settler)
      A helper the agent inside a colony calls for one slice of its work: read these
      files, run the tests, write this one fix. Settlers are short-lived — a colony
      spawns them, they report back to the colony that spawned them, and they die with
      it. A settler never opens a pull request and never outlives its sandbox; the
      colony is the one that talks to you and does the publishing.

  control plane (mothership)
      The `colonizer` process that starts sandboxes, watches them and routes your
      commands to the right one. One runs per machine, and `colonizer` with no
      subcommand at all starts it. Everything else — the cockpit in a browser, every
      client command, the API other tools call — talks to it over `--host`, so the
      same commands work against a control plane on another machine across a tailnet.

  record of one sandbox (session)
      What the control plane keeps about a colony: its id, state, worktree, logs and
      transcript. That id is what `status`, `logs`, `diff`, `ask`, `answer`, `stop` and
      `resume` all take, so `list` is where you find it.

  scheduled prompt (loop)
      A saved prompt plus a cadence that launches a colony on it —
      `colonizer loop create acme/app --name Triage --prompt '...' daily@09:00`.
      Loops are for work that should happen while you are not watching: triage, sweeps,
      anything that would otherwise be a cron job and a forgotten terminal.
